# Copy to apps/board/.env for local development. Values here are for the local
# Docker Compose Postgres only. Real deployments inject these from the cluster.
DATABASE_URL=postgres://rosterdock:rosterdock@localhost:5432/rosterdock
BOARD_PORT=3000
HOST=127.0.0.1
LOG_LEVEL=info

# Local dev login (POST /api/auth/dev-login). Never on in production.
ROSTERDOCK_DEV_LOGIN=1
# Who may create a company: emails and @domains, comma-separated, or * for anyone who signs in. Unset: the
# first person to create one on an empty board owns it and nobody creates another, except with the dev login
# on, which lets anyone sign in as anyone and so create one.
# ROSTERDOCK_COMPANY_CREATORS=you@example.com,@example.com
ROSTERDOCK_COOKIE_SECURE=0
CORS_ORIGIN=http://localhost:5173
# How long a sign-in lasts, in seconds, from when it was made. Default 30 days.
# ROSTERDOCK_SESSION_TTL_SECONDS=2592000
# Requests a minute one client address may make to each sign-in route (the dev login, the OIDC start and
# callback), counted per board replica. Default 60; 0 turns the limit off. Any wider limit belongs at the
# ingress, which must set X-Forwarded-For itself (docs/security.md).
# ROSTERDOCK_SIGNIN_RATE_LIMIT=60

# Object storage: artifacts over 512 KB (up to 8 MB) go here; unset, the board refuses them. MinIO from
# docker compose (the bucket is created on first use). Region defaults to us-east-1; path style is on
# unless S3_FORCE_PATH_STYLE=0 (AWS virtual-hosted buckets).
S3_ENDPOINT=http://localhost:9000
S3_BUCKET=rosterdock
S3_ACCESS_KEY=rosterdock
S3_SECRET_KEY=rosterdock-dev-only
# S3_REGION=us-east-1
# S3_FORCE_PATH_STYLE=1

# Tests connect here to create throwaway databases (must be able to CREATE DATABASE).
ROSTERDOCK_TEST_ADMIN_URL=postgres://rosterdock:rosterdock@localhost:5432/postgres

# Shared secret the worker fleet presents to claim runs (apps/worker). Any long random string locally.
ROSTERDOCK_WORKER_TOKEN=dev-worker-token
# Token the board opens pull requests with after an approved review (a GitHub token with contents and
# pull-requests write on the project repositories). Unset: reviews still approve; no pull request is opened.
# ROSTERDOCK_GITHUB_TOKEN=
# ROSTERDOCK_GITHUB_API=https://api.github.com
# Postgres connections per board replica (the board's pool and the queue's pool each). Default 10.
# ROSTERDOCK_DB_POOL_MAX=10
# A run whose worker has not reported for this long (ms) is interrupted and its task goes back to To do; the
# reaper looks this often (ms). Keep the first well above the worker's heartbeat (15 s).
# ROSTERDOCK_RUN_STALE_AFTER_MS=60000
# ROSTERDOCK_REAPER_INTERVAL_MS=15000
# Peter chases a task that has waited on a teammate this long (ms), once. Still unanswered this long after the
# chase, or waiting on its manager, on Peter or on no teammate at all, it goes to a person (its goal turns amber).
# ROSTERDOCK_CHASE_BLOCKED_FOR_MS=600000
# Peter plans goals with a model when the board has this key (a name in the cluster secret). Unset: Peter
# still plans, by heuristic (research, requirements, build by role and load). The model is Peter's own.
# ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=
# ANTHROPIC_BASE_URL=https://api.anthropic.com
# Peter can also plan through a model CLI where the board runs, on the CLI's own sign-in (`claude auth login`,
# `codex login`), no key needed. Each company picks in Settings > Peter; Automatic takes the first that works:
# the Claude Code CLI, the Codex CLI, then ANTHROPIC_API_KEY. The board looks for `claude` and `codex` on the
# PATH; name another binary here, or set ROSTERDOCK_PETER_CLIS=0 to offer neither.
# ROSTERDOCK_PETER_CLAUDE_BIN=claude
# ROSTERDOCK_PETER_CODEX_BIN=codex
# ROSTERDOCK_PETER_CLIS=1
# How often Peter's loop passes over every company besides on events (ms); 0 disables the loop.
# ROSTERDOCK_PETER_INTERVAL_MS=30000
# Connection credentials are env var NAMES on connections. A company's names resolve from its own
# secrets (Settings > Connections > Secrets, with ROSTERDOCK_SECRET_STORE below) and from the names listed in
# ROSTERDOCK_SHARED_ENV_NAMES, read here at call time; nothing else in this file ever reaches a company. Whoever
# manages a connection in any company can send a shared value to a server of their choosing, so share only
# what every company may hold; the board refuses to start with one of its own names (DATABASE_URL, ROSTERDOCK_*,
# S3_* keys, the model keys) in the list. Name them after the catalog examples, e.g. HUBSPOT_MCP_TOKEN.
# ROSTERDOCK_SHARED_ENV_NAMES=HUBSPOT_MCP_TOKEN,N8N_MCP_TOKEN
# HUBSPOT_MCP_TOKEN=
# The command lines a stdio connection may run, comma-separated. A stdio server runs on this machine as the
# board, so list only what you would let every company's owners and admins run: each line is the command
# and its arguments, matched exactly, and a final * lets a company add arguments (for a filesystem server,
# any folder, this file and the secret store included: pin the folders instead). Unset: no stdio connection
# runs. Each demo's guide (deploy/demo) lists its own lines, such as:
# ROSTERDOCK_STDIO_COMMANDS=npx -y @modelcontextprotocol/server-filesystem@2026.8.31 ~/Lighthouse/drive,npx -y @modelcontextprotocol/server-memory@2026.8.31
# Needs-you notifications: every event that needs a person (a proposed write, an amber goal, a pull
# request to merge) is posted here as one sentence; a Slack incoming webhook works as is. A company can name
# its own instead (Settings), resolved like a connection's credential: its secrets, then the shared names.
# The URL may carry a token, so it is never logged or stored on the board.
# ROSTERDOCK_NOTIFY_WEBHOOK_URL=
# A GitLab token (api scope) so an approved review opens a merge request on a GitLab project; the API root
# names the host repository URLs must carry (default https://gitlab.com/api/v4).
# ROSTERDOCK_GITLAB_TOKEN=
# ROSTERDOCK_GITLAB_API=
# ...or by email: an SMTP URL with the credentials inside (smtp://user:pass@host:587 upgrades with STARTTLS
# when offered; smtps://user:pass@host:465 is TLS from the start; ?plainAuth=1 allows credentials in the
# clear to a relay on localhost), the recipients, comma-separated, and the sender. A company may name its
# own SMTP URL in Settings instead (one of its secrets, or a shared name).
# ROSTERDOCK_NOTIFY_SMTP_URL=
# ROSTERDOCK_NOTIFY_EMAIL_TO=
# ROSTERDOCK_NOTIFY_EMAIL_FROM=
# Sign-in through an OpenID Connect provider. Set the issuer and client id to turn it on; the client
# secret is a name in the cluster secret. The board finds the endpoints from the issuer's discovery
# document. Optional: the name on the button, the scopes, the email domains allowed to sign in, and how
# the secret travels to the token endpoint (basic, the default, or post). At a public provider (Google,
# Microsoft's common tenants, GitLab.com, Apple) the domains are required, or * to let every account in:
# the board will not start there with none.
# ROSTERDOCK_OIDC_ISSUER=https://accounts.google.com
# ROSTERDOCK_OIDC_CLIENT_ID=
# ROSTERDOCK_OIDC_CLIENT_SECRET=
# ROSTERDOCK_OIDC_NAME=Google
# ROSTERDOCK_OIDC_SCOPES=openid email profile
# ROSTERDOCK_OIDC_ALLOWED_DOMAINS=example.com
# ROSTERDOCK_OIDC_TOKEN_AUTH=basic
# Traces: one OpenTelemetry trace per run, posted to an OTLP/HTTP collector when set. The standard
# variables: the base endpoint (/v1/traces is added) or the traces endpoint, headers as k=v pairs (a token
# may live there; it is never logged), and the service name.
# OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_ENDPOINT=http://localhost:4318
# OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_TRACES_ENDPOINT=http://localhost:4318/v1/traces
# OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_HEADERS=
# OTEL_SERVICE_NAME=rosterdock-board
# A bearer token scrapers must present on /metrics (a name in the cluster secret). Unset: open on the board's port.
# ROSTERDOCK_METRICS_TOKEN=
# When a research review opens, the board fetches the public http(s) sources the documents cite and
# gives the reviewer what they say. Set 0 to keep the board off the web.
# ROSTERDOCK_FETCH_SOURCES=1
# The URL people reach the board at, for the sign-in redirect. Unset: taken from the request (behind a
# proxy that sets X-Forwarded-Proto and Host).
# ROSTERDOCK_PUBLIC_URL=https://rosterdock.example.com
# A secret store the board may write to, for the tokens a connection's OAuth flow obtains and the values
# people set per company under Settings > Connections > Secrets: files in a directory here (dev, compose),
# or `kubernetes` in a pod (the chart sets it with board.oauth.enabled). Unset: no store, so a company's
# names resolve from ROSTERDOCK_SHARED_ENV_NAMES alone.
# ROSTERDOCK_SECRET_STORE=file:.data/secrets
# With `kubernetes`, the board takes the API server, its namespace and its token from the pod. These name
# others instead (a board outside the namespace it keeps Secrets in, or a test).
# ROSTERDOCK_KUBERNETES_API=https://kubernetes.default.svc
# ROSTERDOCK_KUBERNETES_NAMESPACE=rosterdock
# ROSTERDOCK_KUBERNETES_TOKEN_FILE=/var/run/secrets/kubernetes.io/serviceaccount/token
